When you start out in the language,
you can barely hear the differences.

00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:06.060
In fact, I would say most people
can't hear the difference.

00:00:06.220 --> 00:00:08.039
Hi there, steve Kaufmann here.

00:00:08.045 --> 00:00:11.400
And, uh, today I want to
talk about Spanish from Spain

00:00:11.610 --> 00:00:13.350
versus Spanish from Mexico.

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:17.850
Uh, remember if you enjoy these videos,
please subscribe, click on the bell for

00:00:17.850 --> 00:00:19.950
notifications when there's a new video.

00:00:20.535 --> 00:00:24.075
Uh, follow me please, uh, as well, uh,
if you wanna practice your English,

00:00:24.075 --> 00:00:27.225
especially on a podcast service,
and please do leave a comment.

00:00:27.645 --> 00:00:31.035
So, first of all, I do have I
getting addressed for my video.

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:33.345
I found a LingQ t-shirt, so
I'm wearing my LingQ T-shirt.

00:00:33.705 --> 00:00:37.215
It comes up all the time:
should I learn the Spanish in

00:00:37.215 --> 00:00:39.555
Spain or the Spanish in Mexico?

00:00:40.589 --> 00:00:45.629
Or I would rather learn the Spanish
in Mexico or the Spanish in Spain?

00:00:45.629 --> 00:00:49.410
Or I don't want to use material
from Spain because I wanna learn

00:00:49.410 --> 00:00:51.390
Spanish, the Spanish from Mexico?

00:00:51.569 --> 00:00:53.280
My take on this is as follows.

00:00:53.819 --> 00:00:58.830
You can find all kinds of material
by Googling, which will give you, you

00:00:58.830 --> 00:01:02.760
know, a hundred differences between
say, Mexican Spanish, and Spanish in

00:01:02.775 --> 00:01:04.650
Spain, and you can get lists of these.

00:01:04.769 --> 00:01:06.270
It's kind of useless, in my opinion.

00:01:06.630 --> 00:01:08.490
The difference doesn't matter.

00:01:08.895 --> 00:01:09.345
Okay.

00:01:09.825 --> 00:01:11.955
It doesn't matter for
a variety of reasons.

00:01:11.985 --> 00:01:16.065
First of all, the Spanish world
is not divided into Mexican

00:01:16.065 --> 00:01:17.475
Spanish, and Spanish in Spain.

00:01:17.685 --> 00:01:21.255
The largest Spanish speaking
countries in the world are Mexico,

00:01:21.255 --> 00:01:25.395
of course, number one, followed by
Argentina and Columbia, I believe.

00:01:25.755 --> 00:01:29.414
And even the United States is a larger
Spanish speaking country than Spain.

00:01:29.414 --> 00:01:33.615
But that's kind of meaningless because
the Spanish speakers in the United States

00:01:33.615 --> 00:01:37.755
have come from somewhere else, so they
don't really represent a particular,

00:01:37.845 --> 00:01:39.824
sort of, unified form of the language.

00:01:40.304 --> 00:01:44.264
Second of all, Spanish, of the languages
where you have, you know, different

00:01:44.264 --> 00:01:48.104
regional accents, which includes
obviously English, with Australian

00:01:48.104 --> 00:01:52.065
English and New Zealand English and,
and, uh, Singapore English and Scottish

00:01:52.065 --> 00:01:56.535
English and, and, uh, you know, Liverpool
English, and, uh, American, which

00:01:56.535 --> 00:02:01.634
is again, not one American, but New
York, Boston, Texas, uh, you name it.

00:02:02.715 --> 00:02:05.745
So in all of these countries
there are differences, but in the

00:02:05.745 --> 00:02:10.065
Spanish, my experience is that
the vowels don't change that much.

00:02:10.155 --> 00:02:14.535
So there's not, it's not that difficult
to understand the different variations.

00:02:14.985 --> 00:02:19.545
The consonants do change in that some
of the places they don't pronounce

00:02:19.545 --> 00:02:21.245
the S for example, the Chileans.

00:02:21.465 --> 00:02:22.945
So there are, there are differences.

00:02:23.315 --> 00:02:28.215
But to me, and, and also I should say
the, the differences in Spanish, just

00:02:28.215 --> 00:02:31.335
as in English or other languages,
it's not just a matter of Spain

00:02:31.335 --> 00:02:35.595
versus Mexico, or even Spain versus
Mexico versus Peru, versus Columbia,

00:02:35.595 --> 00:02:38.355
versus Argentina, Chile, Nicaragua.

00:02:39.285 --> 00:02:42.175
It's also within those countries, there
are tremendous regional differences.

00:02:43.005 --> 00:02:47.655
Uh, I hear this when I listen to a
Columbia Columbian telenovela, differences

00:02:47.655 --> 00:02:52.125
in pronunciation and certainly I had a
discussion with a Columbian who taught

00:02:52.125 --> 00:02:55.845
Spanish online, I can't remember her
name, but she said the regions in Columbia

00:02:55.850 --> 00:03:00.345
where this term is used, where, you
know, getting into to the whole, uh,

00:03:00.925 --> 00:03:03.825
usted and tu as a general rule in Mexico.

00:03:04.410 --> 00:03:09.210
The formal, people tend to use usted more,
which is actually third person, whereas

00:03:09.210 --> 00:03:11.490
in Spain they go to tu more quickly.

00:03:11.850 --> 00:03:15.930
But she was explaining to me that in
Columbia there are parts of the country

00:03:15.935 --> 00:03:20.700
where they use usted more parts of the
country where they use tu more parts of

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:25.410
the country, where they invert and the
more casual, informal form is you state.

00:03:25.650 --> 00:03:29.740
So all of this stuff can vary within
countries from country to country,

00:03:30.690 --> 00:03:32.339
for Spanish and for other languages.

00:03:32.429 --> 00:03:36.530
The point is, though, that you
have to get used to it all.

00:03:37.220 --> 00:03:40.769
If you're watching a movie in Spanish,
it's very possible that there will be

00:03:40.950 --> 00:03:45.600
actors there who are, are characters that
are from Spain or from Mexico, or from

00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:47.970
Columbia, or from Peru, from Argentina.

00:03:48.630 --> 00:03:49.649
Uh, you can't control that.

00:03:49.649 --> 00:03:50.820
You have to understand them all.

00:03:51.149 --> 00:03:53.670
Uh, if you're reading a book, there
could be characters there from

00:03:53.670 --> 00:03:55.260
different Spanish speaking countries.

00:03:56.220 --> 00:03:57.209
You need words.

00:03:58.125 --> 00:04:00.704
Learning a language is primarily
a matter of learning words.

00:04:00.825 --> 00:04:05.385
You should learn these words from
any source that you find interesting,

00:04:06.165 --> 00:04:09.885
and if that happens to be Mexican
or Spanish or Colombian or whatever,

00:04:09.945 --> 00:04:11.445
you shouldn't say, exclude that.

00:04:11.445 --> 00:04:15.555
I don't wanna listen to that because
I wanna focus on whatever Mexican

00:04:15.555 --> 00:04:16.964
Spanish, because I'm going to Mexico.

00:04:17.475 --> 00:04:19.904
You need the words and the
differences aren't that great.

00:04:20.635 --> 00:04:24.765
And when you start out in the language,
you can barely hear the differences.

00:04:24.765 --> 00:04:27.375
In fact, I would say most people
can't hear the difference.

00:04:27.765 --> 00:04:30.075
So just treat it all as one language.

00:04:30.315 --> 00:04:31.724
Read as much as you can.

00:04:31.905 --> 00:04:33.465
Listen as much as you can.

00:04:33.705 --> 00:04:35.265
Acquire the vocabulary.

00:04:35.655 --> 00:04:41.594
And as you stumble across regional
terms, you will learn, Oh yeah, this

00:04:41.594 --> 00:04:44.815
is a term used in, uh, you know,
Columbia to mean something or other,

00:04:45.075 --> 00:04:46.474
it's a very much a regional term.

00:04:47.364 --> 00:04:50.865
You'll gradually, as you get
exposed to more and more of the sort

00:04:51.305 --> 00:04:54.075
ofvernacular of a particular country,
you'll get more and more used to it.

00:04:54.825 --> 00:04:59.294
At some point, if you're gonna be
living in or traveling in Mexico,

00:04:59.294 --> 00:05:04.905
Spain, Columbia, wherever, you may
wanna focus in on a particular sort of

00:05:04.905 --> 00:05:09.585
form of pronunciation because you find
that more attractive or more useful.

00:05:10.185 --> 00:05:16.075
But the likelihood is that even if
you think you are immitating that, you

00:05:16.140 --> 00:05:20.520
know, Mexican Spanish to the local,
you still sound like a foreigner,

00:05:20.820 --> 00:05:23.159
so it won't make much difference.

00:05:23.429 --> 00:05:26.070
The main thing is, can
you put words together?

00:05:26.190 --> 00:05:28.289
Do you understand what people are saying?

00:05:28.650 --> 00:05:32.429
Are you able to express yourself
with words, even with mistakes, even

00:05:32.435 --> 00:05:34.859
with some pronunciation, fuzziness?

00:05:35.070 --> 00:05:35.969
That's the main thing.

00:05:36.330 --> 00:05:39.450
So that this distinction, you
know, do I, I wanna make sure I'm

00:05:39.450 --> 00:05:41.849
learning, you know, uh, whatever.

00:05:42.479 --> 00:05:43.880
To me is, it's not that meaningful.

00:05:44.594 --> 00:05:50.265
If anything in Portuguese, the difference
is greater between, say, the Portuguese

00:05:50.265 --> 00:05:52.815
in Portugal and the Portuguese in Brazil.

00:05:53.085 --> 00:05:56.114
But even within Brazil and within
Portugal, you have regional differences.

00:05:56.715 --> 00:06:01.544
But as a general rule, if you sort of
take Portuguese accents from Portugal,

00:06:01.544 --> 00:06:06.315
Brazilian as Brazilian accents from
Brazil, those are more different.

00:06:06.315 --> 00:06:10.575
And the Portuguese spoken in Portugal
is often held to be more difficult

00:06:10.575 --> 00:06:15.000
to understand for a learner than the
Portuguese spoken in Brazil because

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:19.470
the Portuguese have a tendency to chew
their, uh, syllables a little bit.

00:06:19.590 --> 00:06:24.090
But even there, when I was learning
Portuguese, I wanted to listen to both.

00:06:24.150 --> 00:06:25.860
I want to be able to understand both.

00:06:26.070 --> 00:06:29.940
If I find something interesting
that's in Portuguese podcast from

00:06:29.940 --> 00:06:33.480
Portugal, or a podcast from Brazil,
I want to be able to understand it.

00:06:33.720 --> 00:06:36.090
I want to be able to use it
for my language learning.

00:06:36.540 --> 00:06:40.919
If you are learning English, and you
have a person speaking to you who's very

00:06:40.919 --> 00:06:47.760
interesting and you like his or her voice,
and he or she is Scottish or from Texas

00:06:47.765 --> 00:06:53.820
or Georgia or uh, Liverpool or Australia,
you still wanna access that material.

00:06:54.360 --> 00:06:57.930
So I just think there's far too
much emphasis, similarly with

00:06:57.930 --> 00:07:01.679
French, between the French spoken in
Montreal or in Paris or in Marseille.

00:07:02.280 --> 00:07:06.060
To me, these are not, or Haiti, for that
matter, where the language is a little bit

00:07:06.060 --> 00:07:08.415
different, these are not important issues.

00:07:08.655 --> 00:07:10.005
Get the words in you.

00:07:10.005 --> 00:07:11.595
Get the brain used to the language.

00:07:11.655 --> 00:07:13.095
Speak when you have an opportunity.

00:07:13.095 --> 00:07:15.195
Don't worry about how you sound.

00:07:15.525 --> 00:07:18.525
Go for it and take in the whole language.

00:07:18.585 --> 00:07:25.035
So that's my take on Mexican Spanish
versus Spanish, uh, in Spain.

00:07:25.335 --> 00:07:26.655
It doesn't matter.

00:07:26.835 --> 00:07:29.595
It actually is a false
statement of the problem.

00:07:30.090 --> 00:07:35.550
Spain, Spanish, you know, is
widespread around the world,

00:07:35.910 --> 00:07:37.440
as are many other languages.

00:07:37.500 --> 00:07:39.930
Enjoy them all, all
variations of the language.

00:07:40.260 --> 00:07:44.400
And, and on a final note, these
regional differences in different

00:07:44.400 --> 00:07:49.110
forms of Spanish, it, it's still one
language, so it's not like Spanish and

00:07:49.110 --> 00:07:52.950
Portuguese, which are different languages,
although very, very similar with a high

00:07:52.950 --> 00:07:55.570
degree of, uh, mutual intelligence.

00:07:56.190 --> 00:07:58.020
Vocabulary is very, very similar.

00:07:58.380 --> 00:08:03.060
A similar case would be say the
Scandinavian languages, where

00:08:03.060 --> 00:08:06.090
there's a greater difference in
vocabulary and syntax, but still,

00:08:06.090 --> 00:08:09.539
they kind of more or less understand
each other a lot of the time.

00:08:09.960 --> 00:08:13.440
But they're, all of these are considered
languages, partly because they're separate

00:08:13.440 --> 00:08:18.570
countries and partly because uh, you
know, there's a sufficient difference.

00:08:19.080 --> 00:08:24.420
Um, you know, uh, Schweizerdeutsch in
Switzerland is considered to be a separate

00:08:24.420 --> 00:08:29.310
language, but it's a, it's a form, it's in
effect a dialect of German, but Austrian

00:08:29.310 --> 00:08:30.660
is not considered a separate language.

00:08:30.660 --> 00:08:34.710
Bavarian is not considered a separate
language today, the language that's spoken

00:08:34.710 --> 00:08:36.780
in Bavaria or Austria is still German.

00:08:36.900 --> 00:08:41.580
If we get to say Ukrainian and, and,
and Russian or the Slavic languages,

00:08:41.580 --> 00:08:46.350
they are again, very similar, similar
grammar, a lot of common vocabulary.

00:08:46.350 --> 00:08:50.040
But there again, you know, some lang,
some of the Slavic languages are

00:08:50.040 --> 00:08:51.990
closer to each other than than others.

00:08:51.995 --> 00:08:57.060
So Russian tends to be an outlier from
a vocabulary point of view, whereas

00:08:57.180 --> 00:09:00.690
Ukrainian, Polish, Slovak, et cetera,
Czecg even are closer together.

00:09:01.275 --> 00:09:03.795
But there are separate languages
from a political point of view and

00:09:03.795 --> 00:09:07.365
from a, you know, the extent to
which they are mutually intelligible.

00:09:07.725 --> 00:09:11.505
Uh, so that's a very different
situation from, and, and even with

00:09:11.505 --> 00:09:15.585
Chinese, I mean, you've got say,
Mandarin as spoken in Taiwan versus,

00:09:15.585 --> 00:09:17.055
uh, different areas of the mainland.

00:09:17.060 --> 00:09:19.095
It's still the same language,
it's still Mandarin.

00:09:19.395 --> 00:09:24.795
That's not the same as what is sometimes
called, what are sometimes called regional

00:09:24.795 --> 00:09:28.545
dialects, like Cantonese or Shanghainese,
but which to me are different languages.

00:09:28.860 --> 00:09:33.390
Uh, with essentially
overwhelmingly the same vocabulary.

00:09:33.390 --> 00:09:37.200
But again, once you get into casual
conversation, the vocabulary is different

00:09:37.200 --> 00:09:40.470
and the pronunciation is so different
that they're mutually unintelligible.

00:09:40.560 --> 00:09:44.790
Anyway, a bit of a rant here on this
whole subject of, of, you know, a

00:09:44.790 --> 00:09:46.770
different variance of the language.

00:09:46.775 --> 00:09:49.230
And again, it's not like Arabic
where the language is in fact

00:09:49.230 --> 00:09:50.730
are almost different languages.

00:09:51.315 --> 00:09:51.615
It's...

00:09:51.645 --> 00:09:55.965
the Spanish thing is they're very, very
similar and it doesn't matter which one

00:09:55.965 --> 00:09:59.235
you listen to and which one you use to
improve your Spanish, in my opinion.

00:09:59.445 --> 00:10:00.255
Thanks for listening.

00:10:00.605 --> 00:10:00.945
Bye for now.

